1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
If a game has only one button, can it only do
one thing?

2
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:06,360
Kirby's minigames suggest otherwise. You can
press quickly,

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:10,180
wait for the right moment, or resist pressing
at all.

4
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:12,160
Your finger uses the same button,

5
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:14,680
but the decisions in your head are completely
different.

6
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:17,860
Let's explore four approaches to one-button
play,

7
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:21,680
and how a small set of controls can still
create interesting games.

8
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:25,800
The first approach is button mashing:
competing to

9
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:28,440
press as quickly as possible within a limited
time.

10
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:33,060
The explanation is simple. Each press adds
power, and faster presses take you farther.

11
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:35,980
The screen can communicate the rule before
anyone explains it.

12
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:38,840
But repeating the same movement for a long
time can be tiring,

13
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:41,340
and people differ in how quickly they can
press.

14
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:43,520
So, rather than demanding endless repetition,

15
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:48,460
short bursts of effort separated by rest may
be a better fit.

16
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:50,060
Mashing is not inherently bad.

17
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:54,140
It is an input whose effort and momentary
tension need a purpose.

18
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:59,500
The second approach is timing. In Kirby's
Megaton Punch,

19
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,560
you align power, position, and the final
timing in sequence.

20
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,920
More presses do not mean better play.

21
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:07,420
What matters is reading the motion and waiting
for the right moment.

22
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:11,080
With the same button, a wider target can
create a forgiving activity,

23
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:13,260
while a narrower target can create a precision
challenge.

24
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:17,700
It also helps to show whether a failed press
was slightly early or slightly late.

25
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,680
That tells the player what to change next
time.

26
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:23,760
If they think, 'Ah, I should have waited one
more beat,'

27
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:27,080
they are already preparing for another
attempt.

28
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:31,260
The third approach is reaction. In Samurai
Kirby,

29
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:33,880
you try to press faster than your opponent
after a signal appears.

30
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:36,560
Timing games often ask you to anticipate a
moment.

31
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:40,500
Reaction games focus more on recognizing that
the signal has actually appeared.

32
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:43,820
You want to press quickly, but pressing too
early means failure.

33
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:47,880
Even a wait in which nothing happens can
become tense.

34
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:53,220
Making the signal hard to perceive is not the
same as making the wait exciting.

35
00:01:53,220 --> 00:01:56,060
Use multiple cues rather than relying only on
color or sound,

36
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:59,660
and make the criteria for success and failure
clear.

37
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:03,960
The fourth approach is switching between
holding the button and releasing it.

38
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:09,660
In Kirby's Egg Catcher, you hold for eggs and
release for bombs. Things approach

39
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:13,520
from the same direction, but you must decide
what to accept and what to avoid.

40
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:17,960
This is not a toggle that switches a function
on or off with each press.

41
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:20,160
What matters is whether the button is being
held right now.

42
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,260
The player reads the approaching object and

43
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:25,100
decides whether to maintain or change their
action.

44
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:28,260
You have not hidden lots of functions behind
one button.

45
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:31,960
Two states can already produce different
choices in different situations.

46
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:37,740
Now let's put the same button on two screens.
On the left, only a number changes.

47
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:42,060
On the right, the button moves down, an object
jumps, and the result is easy to read.

48
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:45,860
Even with identical rules, the experience can
change depending on

49
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:48,860
how clearly the game communicates your input
and its consequence.

50
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,820
The same applies to holding a button. A
hold-to-confirm action that prevents

51
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:56,540
mistakes has a different purpose from charging
and firing when you release.

52
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,020
Ask what the player can decide while waiting,

53
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:01,780
and whether choosing when to release creates
an interesting decision.

54
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:06,620
But more screen shake and louder sound do not
automatically make better feedback.

55
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:10,140
First, make the information needed for the
next decision clear.

56
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:14,080
In short: mashing focuses on speed, timing on
a moment,

57
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:17,540
reaction on a signal, and holding or releasing
on a state.

58
00:03:17,740 --> 00:03:20,480
When designing controls, start with what the
player observes and when they decide,

59
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:24,140
rather than how many buttons you need.

60
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:26,380
Then check whether the result is immediately
understandable

61
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:29,540
and whether the player can see why an attempt
failed.

62
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:33,560
One button can be enough. If the reason to
press it is interesting,

63
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:35,580
the game has already begun.
